00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:16.390
POS is the most sensitive spectroscope that we have ever flown in spectroscope.

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:23.390
Spectroscopes or spectrographs are so important for research.

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:29.800
They produce ugly pictures, but they are the nuts and bolts.

00:00:30.190 --> 00:00:31.530
of physical science.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:34.360
They put the physics in astrophysics.

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:39.360
COS was conceived in the mid-1990s by Dr. Jim Green

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:41.960
and his colleagues at the University of Colorado,

00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:46.000
primarily to study the cosmic web, which

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.240
is made up of the largest-scale structures of matter

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:49.200
in the entire universe.

00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:54.850
If you want to know what something is made of,

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:59.360
how hot it is, how dense it is, how fast it's moving in space,

00:00:59.460 --> 00:00:59.960
how fast it's moving in space, how fast it's moving.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:04.240
rotating, for example. A spectrograph will give you all that information. With

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:08.980
costs, we can acquire information like that farther out across the universe

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:20.810
than we've ever been able to do before. Spectroscopy is taking light from an

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:27.050
object and breaking it up into the different colors that that light

00:01:27.050 --> 00:01:29.960
consists of. Each of the elements

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:36.200
Each of the chemical elements has characteristic wavelengths, characteristic colors at which

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:39.780
it emits light when you heat it up or absorbs light.

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:48.790
For example, if I have a tube full of hydrogen between me and that light, instead of seeing

00:01:48.790 --> 00:01:52.770
the normal spectrum of that light when I look at it with the spectrograph, I'll see that

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:58.060
spectrum but with some of the light taken away at the wavelengths where hydrogen has its

00:01:58.060 --> 00:01:59.380
characteristic absorptions.

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:05.680
And so by measuring the depth of those notches and the velocities and the width of them and so on,

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:09.800
you can infer all kinds of things about the physical state of that cloud.

00:02:10.450 --> 00:02:14.950
COS has taken a really key part of spectroscopic science and said,

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:18.750
how can we do that in the absolutely best, most efficient way?

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:28.920
And that is to measure the properties of the material between the galaxies looking back into the universe.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.740
galaxies form there's a lot of material that does not collapse into the galaxies

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:39.640
and there's other material that is ejected from galaxies by supernova

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:43.180
explosions and so on and so that intergalactic gas the so-called

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:47.390
intergalactic medium carries a lot of information about the history of the

00:02:47.390 --> 00:02:53.640
universe when you couple that story sort of the global cosmic process of how you

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:57.640
form the large-scale structure of how material is distributed in the universe

00:02:57.640 --> 00:02:59.970
and what role that played in forming new games.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:05.780
galaxies, and then you use Wide Field Camera 3 to investigate how did the galaxies themselves

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:12.660
change internally with time and over space, looking back through the history of the universe.

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:19.000
All that kind of ties together into the full story of where we came from.

